From 71c9daa8ec11401459f8fb6fbd3e4acd8ad88cc0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arealjim <6187715+arealjim@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 14:16:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/23] runtime: pace each frame's UDP packets over
 send_pacing_ms

A frame handed to the socket at once leaves as a line-rate burst of 10-200
datagrams; an access point queue under contention drops the tail, and the
XOR FEC (one parity per 10) cannot repair consecutive losses. PacketPacer
spreads a frame's data and parity datagrams evenly over a window
(send_pacing_ms, keyframes send_pacing_keyframe_ms or twice that), capped at
0.8 of the frame period, and sends the rest at once when a newer frame is
queued. Waits spin below 1 ms, so the only latency added is the window.
Default 0 keeps the old burst. Config is re-read every 90 frames, so a toml
reload switches it live.

sendto failures were silent; they are now counted (udpSendFail) with the
pacing state, flushes and wake lateness at the end of the encode-queue line.

oxrsys_pacing_probe sends a synthetic 90 fps, 50 Mbps stream packetised like
SendNalUnit to 127.0.0.1 only and reports burst span, packet gaps, peak 1 ms
rate and added latency. Measured on the M5 Pro, Debug, loopback, 5 s:
burst: P span p50 0.95 ms, peak 1 ms rate p99 684 Mbps, max 843
4/8 ms: P span p50 3.88 ms, peak 1 ms rate p99 342 Mbps, max 444,
wake lateness p99 0.25 ms, CPU user 0.41 -> 1.74 s per 5 s.

